1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
August 2003. A pizza delivery man
walks into a bank in Pennsylvania —

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:08,000
— with a live bomb locked around his neck.

3
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:12,500
Brian Wells was an ordinary delivery driver.

4
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:17,000
That afternoon, an order came in —
to an empty TV tower site outside town.

5
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:21,500
There, a device was locked onto his neck.

6
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:26,000
He was ordered to rob a bank and follow
a set of written instructions.

7
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
He later told police he was forced.

8
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:33,000
He walked out with about $8,700.

9
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:36,500
But this was only the first step.

10
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:40,500
More instructions, like a scavenger hunt,
were waiting for him.

11
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Minutes after leaving the bank,
police stopped him.

12
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,500
They waited for the bomb squad.
There wasn't enough time.

13
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,500
On live camera, the device went off.

14
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:56,500
Brian Wells died, still waiting
for someone to save him.

15
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,000
The investigation took years.

16
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,000
At its center was Marjorie Diehl-Armstrong,

17
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:08,500
who had a documented history of mental illness,
and an earlier death linked to her.

18
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:13,000
In 2007, accomplice Kenneth Barnes
pleaded guilty.

19
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:18,500
In 2010, Diehl-Armstrong was sentenced to 45 years
for conspiracy and related charges.

20
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:22,000
She died in prison in 2017.

21
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,500
Another figure, William Rothstein,
died of illness before trial.

22
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:31,500
Whether Wells was an accomplice
or a victim is still debated.

23
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:36,000
What we know is that he was waiting
for help until the very end.
